xnu 0401: the IOVA arena keeps every freed range, and a refusal names its step

A boot left running for three hours measured 9 479
`MondrianGPU: gem_new 0x1400000 refused` (0xa00000, 0x700000 and 0x500000
behind it), the first one at 10 614 s of uptime, while 6 GiB of RAM were free
and 282 GPU buffers were live. Only large buffers were refused: the arena's
bump pointer had reached the end of the 1.75 GiB window and the 128 remembered
holes still had room for small allocations, so surfaces and textures stopped
being mappable and the compositor fell to 1-2 frames a second. Nothing in the
ring said which step had refused, so the size read as "out of memory".

The hole list now stays sorted and coalesces with both neighbours, so it holds
one range per boundary between live buffers instead of one per free() ever
seen; 1 024 entries, and a range it cannot record is counted in `dropped`
instead of disappearing. `mondrian_gpu_uc_iova_state()` prints the bump, the
hole count, the largest hole and the dropped bytes with every refusal, and the
user client names the step (`no memory`, `arena`, `map`, `no page`,
`descriptor`, `buffer count`, `too big`).

